# All You Need to Run the qaas Harness

Practical runbook for `allaabdellawebsite`. Every path and value here is the real
one for this machine.

---

## 0. The 30-second version

```bash
# Terminal 1 — the app must be running
cd ~/Documents/allaabdellawebsite/project/react-site && npm run dev

# Terminal 2 — the run
cd ~/Documents/allaabdellawebsite
qaas run --mode pr-check          # ~15 min, finds + files
qaas run --mode full-loop         # ~3 hrs, finds + files + fixes + verifies

# Terminal 3 — watch it
cd ~/Documents/allaabdellawebsite
qaas runs                                    # newest run is the TOP row
qaas trace <run-id> --follow --quiet

# After
qaas show <run-id>
qaas board
```

---

## 1. What `.qaas/` is, and where it lives

`.qaas/` is **runtime state + your local config**. It is created by `qaas init`
and lives at the **root of the directory you run qaas from** — *not* inside your
source code.

```
~/Documents/allaabdellawebsite/          ← you run qaas from HERE
├── .qaas/                               ← state + config  (NOT in git: outside the repo)
│   ├── .env                             ← credentials (chmod 600)
│   ├── config/
│   │   ├── system.yaml                  ← tracker, vcs, thresholds, run modes
│   │   ├── targets/
│   │   │   └── allaabdellawebsite.yaml  ← WHAT is under test (root, env, layout)
│   │   └── agents/
│   │       └── fixer.yaml               ← per-agent overrides (shadows packaged)
│   ├── runs/<run-id>/
│   │   ├── ledger.jsonl                 ← every event, append-only
│   │   ├── envelopes/                   ← the findings, one JSON each
│   │   ├── artifacts/                   ← screenshots, traces, agent tasks
│   │   └── results/                     ← per-agent cost, turns, duration
│   ├── tickets/                         ← only when tracker: local
│   └── system-map/                      ← MAPPER's output, shared across runs
│
└── project/react-site/                  ← THE TARGET (this is the git repo)
    ├── App.jsx  Editor.jsx  Services.jsx …
    ├── src/
    └── qa/repro/                        ← REPRODUCER's sandbox
```

**Important:** your git repo is `project/react-site`, and `.qaas/` sits *above*
it — so credentials can never be committed. If you ever move `.qaas/` inside a
repo, add `.qaas/` to that repo's `.gitignore` first.

---

## 2. Set the target path (the #1 thing people get wrong)

`root:` tells qaas where the application actually is. **Everything else —
write paths, test sandboxes, layout hints — is relative to it.**

```bash
cd ~/Documents/allaabdellawebsite
cat .qaas/config/targets/allaabdellawebsite.yaml
```

Change it with one command:

```bash
cp .qaas/config/targets/allaabdellawebsite.yaml{,.bak}
sed -i '' 's|^root:.*|root: project/react-site|' .qaas/config/targets/allaabdellawebsite.yaml
qaas targets        # the "root" column must show project/react-site
```

**Current correct value:** `root: project/react-site`

### Why it matters

REPRODUCER's sandbox is `qa/repro`, resolved as `<root>/qa/repro`.
With `root: .` that was `allaabdellawebsite/qa/repro` — the wrong place — so
every failing test it tried to write was **refused**, no evidence existed, and
**zero tickets were filed**. With `root: project/react-site` it resolves to
`project/react-site/qa/repro`, which is correct.

---

## 3. Change the environment mode (unlocks the browser agents)

Three modes, in `.qaas/config/targets/allaabdellawebsite.yaml`:

| mode | meaning |
|---|---|
| `none` | no running app. Static code reading only. |
| `external` | the app is already running (your dev server). Agents may read and **exercise** it, never reset it. ← **use this** |
| `compose` | qaas brings the app up itself via docker-compose and owns its lifecycle. |

Edit the block:

```bash
${EDITOR:-nano} .qaas/config/targets/allaabdellawebsite.yaml
```

```yaml
environment:
  mode: external
  web_url: http://localhost:5173      # your Vite dev server
  api_url: http://localhost:5173
  health_path: /                      # "/health" 404s on a plain Vite SPA
  services: []
  startup_timeout_s: 180
```

Then:

```bash
qaas doctor --target allaabdellawebsite
```

`live_ui` and `live_api` must read **yes**. They flip only when mode is
`external`/`compose` **and** the URLs are set.

### The layout block

Layout paths are also root-relative and are injected into each agent's task
prompt as a *hint*:

```yaml
layout:
  frontend: [.]        # JSX lives at the root of project/react-site
  backend: []
  tests: []
  exclude: [node_modules, dist, build, .git, coverage, ...]
```

---

## 4. Connect Jira

### Credentials

Put them in `.qaas/.env` (never in a YAML, never in git):

```bash
cat > .qaas/.env <<'EOF'
JIRA_BASE_URL=https://allaabdella2-1765419655665.atlassian.net
JIRA_EMAIL=allaabdella2@gmail.com
JIRA_API_TOKEN=<your-token>
JIRA_PROJECT_KEY=QAAS
QAAS_TRACKER=jira
EOF
chmod 600 .qaas/.env
```

Rules:
- `.qaas/.env` is read automatically before **every** command.
- **Anything exported in your shell wins** over the file — so a stale `.env`
  can never silently redirect a run.
- `QAAS_ENV_FILE=` (empty) disables the file entirely.

### Switch the tracker backend

Two ways, both valid:

```bash
# A. permanent, in .qaas/.env
QAAS_TRACKER=jira

# B. per-shell, overrides everything
QAAS_TRACKER=jira qaas run --mode pr-check
```

Backends: `local` (JSON files in `.qaas/tickets/`, default, nothing leaves the
machine) or `jira`.

### Verify — creates nothing

```bash
qaas tracker-check
```

Must show:
```
tracker backend: jira
auth              ok   authenticated as Alla Abdella
project QAAS      ok
permissions QAAS  ok   Browse, Create, Transition, Link
issue type QAAS   ok   'Bug' exists in QAAS
```

Prove it works without your shell exports (i.e. in a fresh terminal):

```bash
env -u JIRA_API_TOKEN -u JIRA_BASE_URL -u JIRA_EMAIL -u JIRA_PROJECT_KEY qaas tracker-check
```

### Known gaps in this Jira project

| gap | consequence |
|---|---|
| `JIRA_SECURITY_PROJECT_KEY` unset | Security findings (Firestore rules, auth flags) are **REFUSED, not filed** — they escalate to you instead. Set it to a restricted project to file them. |
| `in_review` → *no match* | The QAAS workflow has Backlog / In Progress / Done only. REVIEWER's transition to `in_review` will raise `TrackerError` mid-loop. Add an "In Review" status, or expect that error. |

### The board

```bash
qaas board              # find or create this repo's view, print the URL
qaas board --no-create  # just show the label + JQL, touch nothing
```

Every ticket is stamped `repo-allaabdellawebsite` **in code**, and a saved filter
over that label is the per-repo view. A real board (To Do / In Progress / Done)
is only created on a **company-managed** Jira project; team-managed (next-gen)
projects own their own board and you get the filter alone. Either way the link
works. The board is provisioned **before the first agent runs**, so you have
something to watch during the run.

---

## 5. Paths, policy, and where each agent may write

Every agent has a `policy:` block. All paths are **relative to the target root**
(`project/react-site`).

| agent | may write to | branches |
|---|---|---|
| MAPPER, ARCHITECT, API, BROWSER, DBA, AUDITOR, SOCKET, GUIDE, LOAD, REVIEWER, REPORTER | **nothing** — read-only | — |
| REPRODUCER | `qa/repro` | `qa/repro/*` |
| **FIXER** | `*.jsx`, `*.css`, `src`, `hooks.jsx`, `qa/repro` | `fix/*` |
| TRIAGE | no files; may create ≤10 tickets/run | — |
| VERIFIER | no files; may transition tickets | — |

### FIXER was overridden for this project

The shipped default is `[api/app, web/src, qa/repro]` — the demo app's layout.
Your JSX is at the root of `project/react-site`, so FIXER would have been denied
on every fix. The override lives at `.qaas/config/agents/fixer.yaml` and
**shadows the packaged file entirely** (it is not merged — copy the whole file
and edit it).

```bash
cat .qaas/config/agents/fixer.yaml
qaas validate          # the FIXER "writes" column shows the effective paths
```

### forbidden_paths — escalate, never auto-fix

Even inside its sandbox, FIXER is refused on: `*auth*`, `*payment*`, `*billing*`,
`*secret*`, `*credential*`, `*migration*`, `*.tf`, `*infra/*`, `Dockerfile*`,
`*.github/*`. These stop at a human however small the change looks. They come
back as **escalations**, not fixes.

### Other rails, enforced in code

- `max_diff_files: 5`, `max_diff_lines: 150` per FIXER run.
- REPRODUCER's failing test is **immutable** — FIXER may not edit the test that
  defines the bug.
- `git push -f`, pushing to `main`, `git merge`, `gh pr merge`, `rm -rf`, `sudo`
  are refused for every agent, always.
- **Merge is impossible.** FIXER opens a **draft PR**. Shipping is always yours.
- A denial returns a reason and is logged — it never kills the agent's turn.

---

## 6. MCP servers — including Playwright

Seven servers run **in-process** (no subprocess): `envelope`, `test_runner`,
`env_control`, `defect_memory`, `contract_diff`, `tracker`, `vcs`.
**Playwright is the one external server**, spawned over stdio.

### How Playwright is activated — the full chain

You never start it manually. It launches automatically when **all four** are true:

1. **The agent declares it.** `browser.yaml` has
   `mcp_servers: [envelope, env_control, playwright]`; `guide.yaml` has it too.
2. **The target has `live_ui`.** `agent_usable()` returns `False` for BROWSER and
   GUIDE unless `live_ui: yes` — so with `mode: none` they are **not dispatched
   at all** (deliberately: watching an agent hunt for a browser that was never
   there is worse than being told it can't run).
3. **The run mode includes them.** `pr-check` has BROWSER; `nightly` and
   `full-loop` have BROWSER **and** GUIDE.
4. **`npx` can fetch it.** qaas spawns:
   ```
   npx -y @playwright/mcp@latest --isolated --browser chromium
   ```

Then the agent is handed `{web_url}` in its task and drives the real browser —
clicking, filling forms, reading console errors, checking a11y — exactly as a
person would. `--isolated` means a fresh profile each run, so nothing persists
between runs.

### One-time prerequisite

```bash
npx playwright install chromium
```

✅ Already installed on this machine (`~/Library/Caches/ms-playwright/chromium-1243`).

### Confirm it will run

```bash
qaas doctor --target allaabdellawebsite
# "agents that can work here:" must include BROWSER and GUIDE
```

If BROWSER appears under *"agents that cannot"*, the cause is always the same:
`live_ui: no` → your `environment.mode` or `web_url` is wrong, or the dev server
isn't running.

### Watch it work

```bash
qaas trace <run-id> --agent BROWSER
```

You'll see `mcp__playwright__browser_navigate`, `browser_click`, `browser_snapshot`
calls in sequence. Screenshots land in `.qaas/runs/<run-id>/artifacts/`.

### Adding your own MCP server

Declare it in `.qaas/config/system.yaml`, then name it in an agent's
`mcp_servers:`. Declaring alone grants nothing — an agent only gets a server it
names. Max 6 servers per agent (tool-selection accuracy).

```yaml
mcp_servers:
  my-server:
    command: npx
    args: ["-y", "@scope/my-mcp-server"]
    env:
      API_KEY: ${MY_API_KEY}
```

---

## 7. Run modes — pick the right one

| mode | agents | wall clock | fixes bugs? |
|---|---|---|---|
| `incident` | API only | 600s | no (no filing at all) |
| `pr-check` | 8 — MAPPER, ARCHITECT, API, BROWSER, DBA, AUDITOR, REPRODUCER, TRIAGE | 900s | **no** |
| `nightly` | 12 — adds SOCKET, GUIDE, LOAD, REPORTER | 7200s | **no** |
| `fix-cycle` | VERIFIER, FIXER, REVIEWER | 3600s | yes, on existing tickets |
| `full-loop` | all 15 | 10800s | **yes — the only mode that closes the loop** |

**`pr-check` and `nightly` do not contain FIXER, REVIEWER or VERIFIER.** They
find and file. If you want tickets to move to Done, you need `full-loop`
(or `fix-cycle` on tickets you already have).

Raise a cap if you hit it — the last run died at `1450s of 900s`:

```yaml
# .qaas/config/system.yaml
run_modes:
  pr-check:
    max_wall_clock_s: 1800
```

---

## 8. The full run, terminal by terminal

### Terminal 1 — the application
```bash
cd ~/Documents/allaabdellawebsite/project/react-site
npm run dev
# leave running. Confirm http://localhost:5173 loads in a browser.
```

### Terminal 2 — pre-flight, then run
```bash
cd ~/Documents/allaabdellawebsite

qaas targets                              # root = project/react-site
qaas doctor --target allaabdellawebsite   # live_ui yes, live_api yes, "ready"
qaas validate                             # "config ok"
qaas tracker-check                        # "tracker backend: jira", auth ok
qaas run --mode pr-check --dry-run        # the plan, no API call, no cost

qaas run --mode pr-check                  # the real run
```

### Terminal 3 — live monitoring
```bash
cd ~/Documents/allaabdellawebsite

qaas runs                                 # newest run is the TOP row
qaas trace <run-id> --follow --quiet      # decisions only, tails live
```

More views of the same live run:
```bash
qaas trace <run-id> --follow              # every file read too (verbose)
qaas trace <run-id> --agent BROWSER       # one agent
qaas trace <run-id> --kind envelope       # findings as they appear
qaas trace <run-id> --kind denial         # what was refused, and why
```

### After it finishes
```bash
qaas show <run-id>        # findings, tickets, verdicts, escalations, denials
qaas board                # the Jira board URL
qaas trace <run-id> --kind ticket
qaas trace <run-id> --kind verdict        # VERIFIED / NOT_FIXED / REGRESSED
qaas trace <run-id> --json > run.json
```

### Then close the loop
```bash
qaas run --mode full-loop
```

